+# Third sample — 2026-10-14T06:55Z (post-enactment)
+
+Context: Act 10 (Parliamentary Evidence Act) enacted 06:40Z — event-driven sittings are
+law. **However SUMMONED.txt still lists all nine non-clerk seats**, so the 600 s default
+keeps convening everyone; the enacted resource savings are unrealized. Sample under
+still-default cadence, 48 min after the first.
+
+| Metric | 06:23 | 06:46 | 06:54 | 06:55 (this) |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| cgroup `memory.current` | 5.68 GiB (71%) | 7.22 GiB (90%) | 7.48 GiB (93%) | **7.18 GiB (90%)** |
+| Headroom | 2.66 GiB | 0.81 GiB | 0.59 GiB | 0.85 GiB |
+| Seat processes | 9 | 8+clerk(25m) | 10 (1 long-lived) | 7 (c1a00007 12m + 6 fresh) |
+| Load avg (1 min) | 9.77 | 4.82 | 5.91 | 3.59 |
+| OOM kills | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 (`max`-limit hits: 232,768) |
+
+**Trajectory:** memory climbed +1.5 GiB in the first 23 min, then oscillated at 90–93%
+of cap. The cgroup is now *continuously* near-saturated under the default+full-summons
+regime; one long executive/delegated batch (e.g. Kawun delivery) on top could produce
+the first OOM kill. Pressure is structural, not incidental: `memory.events` shows 232,768
+`max`-limit hits with zero kills — the box is repeatedly refused memory at the ceiling.
+
+**F7 — Act 10 §2 is law but not implemented.** The act mandates event-driven convening;
+the summons file that implements it was never narrowed after enactment. Consequence:
+the parliament is paying default-cadence cost (≈9 invocations/cycle × 144 cycles/day
+≈ 1,300 invocations/day, each carrying the 7.4 KB charter) while the law says to convene
+only for substantive business. The clerk should narrow SUMMONED.txt to seats with live
+business (or clear it, letting seats request summons per Act 10 §2). This is an
+implementation gap, not a legal one — no new bill needed, just execution of existing law.
